<p>A denied insurance claim in Port Arthur often arrives at the worst possible time: after a hurricane, while the roof is tarped and the drywall is already cut out. The letter may cite policy language that reads like a foreign language. It is easy to assume the decision is final. Often, it is not the last word.</p>

<p>This guide explains how to read a denial letter, the most common reasons claims are denied along the upper Texas coast, practical steps to respond, how a TDI complaint works, when appraisal fits, and when to talk to an attorney. It is general information, not legal advice. Your policy and the facts of your loss control the outcome.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Read the denial letter carefully</h2>

<p>A denial letter should tell you what the insurer decided and why. Read it slowly, ideally with your policy open beside it, and pull out these pieces:</p>

<ul>
<li><strong>Full or partial denial.</strong> Many hurricane claims are partly paid and partly denied. Know exactly which items were rejected.</li>
<li><strong>The stated reason.</strong> Wear and tear? Flood? Late notice? No storm-created opening?</li>
<li><strong>The policy provision.</strong> Most letters quote or cite an exclusion, condition, or limitation by section. Find it in your policy and read the full section, including any exceptions.</li>
<li><strong>The evidence relied on.</strong> An adjuster inspection, an engineer's report, weather data, photos?</li>
<li><strong>Any deadline or next step</strong> the letter mentions.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/homeowner-reviewing-claim-letter.webp" alt="Homeowner reading an insurance claim letter with a policy document nearby" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Match the reason in the letter to the exact policy section it cites, then read the exceptions in that section.</figcaption></figure>

<p>If the letter is vague, write back and ask for the specific policy provisions and the factual basis for the decision. Under Texas prompt payment rules in Insurance Code Chapter 542, an insurer that rejects a claim must notify the claimant in writing of the reasons. A clear written reason makes it much easier to respond.</p>

<h2>Common denial reasons on the Gulf Coast</h2>

<p>Certain reasons come up again and again on Southeast Texas property claims. Here is what each usually means and the evidence that may help answer it.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Denial reason</th><th>What the insurer is saying</th><th>Evidence that may help</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Wear and tear / deterioration</td><td>Damage is from age or lack of maintenance, not the storm</td><td>Pre-storm photos, roof age records, storm-date weather data, signs of fresh damage (creases, torn mats, missing tabs)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Flood / surface water exclusion</td><td>Water rose from outside, which the policy excludes</td><td>Damage above the water line, roof and window openings, timeline, moisture maps</td></tr>
<tr><td>No storm-created opening</td><td>Rain got in without wind first creating an opening</td><td>Photos of missing shingles, lifted flashing, broken windows tied to the date</td></tr>
<tr><td>Late notice</td><td>You reported too long after the loss, hurting the investigation</td><td>When you discovered the damage, why it was hidden, prompt action once found</td></tr>
<tr><td>Pre-existing damage / prior claim</td><td>Damage existed before this storm or was already paid</td><td>Prior repair invoices, photos after earlier repairs, inspection reports</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mold limitation or exclusion</td><td>Mold is excluded or capped at a low sublimit</td><td>Evidence mold resulted from a covered water loss; mitigation timeline</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continuous or repeated leakage</td><td>Water leaked over weeks or months</td><td>Plumber report on the failure, date of discovery, sudden-failure evidence</td></tr>
<tr><td>Below deductible</td><td>The estimate does not exceed the deductible</td><td>A detailed independent estimate showing missed scope</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Wear and tear on older roofs</h3>
<p>Many Port Arthur neighborhoods have older housing stock and composition shingle roofs that have weathered years of Gulf heat and UV. Insurers often point to granule loss, curling, or old repairs and call the damage "wear and tear." The key question is whether the storm caused new, distinct damage on top of the age. Fresh creases, torn shingles, missing tabs with clean edges, and impact marks from hail are different from gradual aging. Our <a href="/services/roof-damage-insurance-claims/">roof damage insurance claims</a> page covers what adjusters look for.</p>

<h3>The flood exclusion after hurricanes</h3>
<p>After a hurricane or a rain event like Harvey or Imelda, homeowners and TWIA policies often deny interior damage as flood. Sometimes that is correct; rising water is excluded and belongs to a flood policy. Sometimes wind or wind-driven rain caused part of the damage. Separating the two with evidence is the core of these disputes. See <a href="/wind-vs-flood-damage-port-arthur-insurance/">wind vs. flood damage in Port Arthur</a>.</p>

<h3>Late notice</h3>
<p>Policies require prompt notice. TWIA says the deadline to report a claim is one year from the date of damage, with a possible extension for good cause. Other policies use phrases like "prompt notice" without a fixed number of days. A denial for late notice may be harder to answer, but the facts matter: when the damage was discovered, whether it was hidden, and whether the delay actually hurt the insurer's ability to investigate. These are often legal questions for an attorney.</p>

<h3>Mold limits</h3>
<p>Southeast Texas humidity means mold can follow a water loss within days. Many policies exclude mold or cap it at a sublimit, but may cover mold that results from a covered water loss up to that limit. Read the mold provisions closely. Our <a href="/services/mold-damage-claims/">mold damage claims</a> page explains how those claims are usually handled.</p>

