<h2>Step 2: Gather the insurer's file and your own evidence</h2>

<p>Before responding, get the full picture:</p>

<ol>
<li><strong>Request the insurer's documents.</strong> Ask in writing for the adjuster's estimate, inspection photos, any engineer or expert report, and the complete policy with all endorsements.</li>
<li><strong>Organize your own evidence.</strong> Photos and video, mitigation reports and moisture logs, receipts, prior repair records, and your communication log. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-document-storm-damage-port-arthur/">how to document storm damage</a> covers what to collect.</li>
<li><strong>Pull weather records.</strong> NOAA and NWS records for your date of loss can support that damaging wind, hail, or flooding occurred at your location. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> is a starting point.</li>
<li><strong>Get an independent opinion where useful.</strong> A roofer's report, plumber's report, or engineer's opinion can address the specific reason for denial.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Step 3: Respond in writing</h2>

<p>A strong response is specific, organized, and tied to the denial reason. It typically includes:</p>

<ul>
<li>Your claim number, policy number, and date of loss.</li>
<li>The reason given for denial, quoted from the letter.</li>
<li>Why you believe the reason does not apply, point by point.</li>
<li>The evidence that supports your position, labeled and attached.</li>
<li>A request for reconsideration and, if helpful, a reinspection with you or your representative present.</li>
</ul>

<p>Keep copies of everything you send and note the date. Stay factual and professional. Insurers often respond better to a well-documented file than to frustration, and the record may matter later.</p>

<h2>Step 4: Consider a TDI complaint</h2>

<p>The Texas Department of Insurance helps consumers with complaints against insurance companies, agents, and adjusters. You can file online at TDI's website, choosing the type of insurance (including Homeowners, TWIA, and Flood), or call the TDI Help Line at 800-252-3439, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Central time, Monday through Friday.</p>

<p>A complaint typically prompts the insurer to respond to TDI about your claim. It can be a useful step when communication has broken down or deadlines are not being met. It is not a substitute for a coverage decision by a court, and it does not guarantee payment. Include your claim number, a short timeline, and copies of key documents.</p>

<h2>Step 5: Appraisal vs. coverage disputes</h2>

<p>This distinction trips up many policyholders.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Amount-of-loss dispute</th><th>Coverage dispute</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Example</td><td>Insurer agrees the roof was damaged by wind but pays for repairs, not replacement</td><td>Insurer says the damage is wear and tear or flood, so nothing is owed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical path</td><td>Negotiation, supplements, appraisal</td><td>Reconsideration, TDI complaint, attorney</td></tr>
<tr><td>Who decides</td><td>Two appraisers and, if needed, an umpire (under the policy's appraisal clause)</td><td>Ultimately a court, if not resolved</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Under a typical appraisal clause, either side can demand appraisal when you disagree about the amount of loss. Each side picks an appraiser, the appraisers pick an umpire, and an award agreed by two of the three is generally binding on the amount. Appraisal usually does not decide whether the loss is covered. If your claim was denied outright as excluded, appraisal is often not the right tool. If coverage was accepted but the payment is low, it may be. Policy language controls. Learn more on our <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a> page.</p>

<figure><img src="/assets/img/photos/insurance-dispute-appraisal.webp" alt="Policyholder and representatives discussing an insurance claim dispute at a table" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Appraisal can resolve disagreements about the amount of a covered loss. Coverage denials usually need a different path.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>When to call a Texas attorney</h2>

<p>Public adjusters are not attorneys. Talk to a Texas attorney who handles insurance disputes when:</p>

<ul>
<li>The denial is based on a coverage exclusion you believe does not apply, and reconsideration has failed.</li>
<li>You are close to a contractual deadline to sue. Many policies use two years and one day; check your policy.</li>
<li>The insurer relies on late notice, misrepresentation, or fraud allegations.</li>
<li>You believe the insurer acted in bad faith or violated the prompt payment statute.</li>
<li>The claim involves TWIA, which has special dispute and litigation rules.</li>
<li>You are considering a lawsuit on a weather-related claim, where Texas law requires written pre-suit notice under Chapter 542A.</li>
</ul>

<p>Do not wait until the last minute. Deadlines can be strict, and an attorney needs time to review the file.</p>

<h2>How a Port Arthur Public Adjuster can help after a denial</h2>

<p>A licensed public adjuster represents the policyholder in the claim process. After a denial, that can mean reviewing the policy and denial letter, re-inspecting and documenting the property, pulling weather data, preparing a detailed estimate, and presenting a supported request for reconsideration. When the dispute is about amount, a public adjuster may also help with supplements or appraisal. When it becomes a legal dispute, we recommend involving a Texas attorney.</p>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement, and Texas contracts can be rescinded within 72 hours of signing. No public adjuster can guarantee that a denial will be reversed. A Port Arthur Public Adjuster review can help you understand whether the denial reason fits the facts. Start with our <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> page or request a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a>. If your claim was paid but the amount seems low, see <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a>.</p>
